After the convolutions there is one feature value per filter per position, and the number of positions depends on the sentence length. Global pooling (usually max, sometimes mean) takes, for each filter, the max/average over all positions. This:
• turns a variable-length sequence into a fixed-size vector (one value per filter) that the final linear + softmax layer can use;
• makes the representation independent of where a pattern occurred (position-invariant: 'did this pattern appear anywhere?');
• summarises the whole context into the single context vector h used to predict the next token.

Note: the lecturer pointed out the cost for generation: every new token means re-running the convolutions and the pooling over the whole sequence, unlike an RNN's running hidden state.
